# Normalise line endings to LF everywhere. Every tracked file is consumed on
# Linux (the AIchor image, the K8s manifest, the shell entrypoint), and none of
# them needs CRLF, so there is nothing here to exempt.
#
# run_pipeline.sh is the file that actually breaks: a CRLF shebang makes bash
# fail with "$'\r': command not found".
#
# eol=lf covers the working tree as well as the repository, which matters
# because "aichor submit local" zips the working tree rather than git, so a
# checked-out CRLF file rides straight into the image. Dockerfile.aichor strips
# CRLF anyway as a second line of defence; keep that line.
#
# Setting eol does not rewrite files that are already checked out. After
# changing this file, run "git checkout -- ." to bring the working tree into
# line with it.

* text=auto eol=lf
